theory.intro:
The three sides of a right-angled triangle are named from one chosen acute angle. Two of those names swap if you work from the other acute angle, so always start by finding the marked angle.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1]
\coordinate (P) at (0,0);
\coordinate (Q) at (4.2,0);
\coordinate (R) at (0,3.2);
\draw (P)--(Q) node[midway,below] {Adjacent};
\draw (Q)--(R) node[midway,above right] {Hypotenuse};
\draw (R)--(P) node[midway,left] {Opposite};
\coordinate (ra1) at ($(P)!0.32cm!(Q)$);
\coordinate (ra2) at ($(P)!0.32cm!(R)$);
\draw (ra1)--($(ra1)+(ra2)-(P)$)--(ra2);
\pic[draw,"$\theta$",angle radius=0.62cm,angle eccentricity=1.75] {angle=R--Q--P};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

facts:
Mark the other acute angle instead and the opposite and adjacent sides swap, while the hypotenuse stays the same side.
